WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.060
This shot from "1917" shows
Lance Corporal Schofield,

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.817
played by George MacKay,

00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:07.220
running through a destroyed town

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.000
and jumping off a high ledge into a river.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.130
It seems like just one
long, continuous take.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.670
However, if you look behind
the scenes leading up to this,

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.720
the camera crew had to chase after George

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.320
by jeep, motorcycle, and even by foot

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.720
as the actor then jumped
off a ledge onto a mat,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.670
which was cobbled
together with another shot

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.370
of the character jumping into a river.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:32.420
This is just one of the many crafty ways

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.020
the filmmakers made the movie look

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.890
like it is all just one single shot.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.280
Getting it right required precise timing,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.250
the highest level of planning,

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.117
and some incredibly clever
shooting and editing.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.390
The entirety of "1917,"

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.506
a drama set during World War I,

00:00:55.506 --> 00:00:56.920
follows a pair of young soldiers

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.770
trying to deliver a
message to stop an attack.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.130
It was not actually shot in one take,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.100
but rather a series of
continuous, uncut shots

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.614
that were then cleverly connected

00:01:06.614 --> 00:01:09.270
to give the feeling of one long take.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.120
While this has been done before,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.940
"1917" presented many new
challenges for the filmmakers.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.537
One of the earliest examples
of a "one shot" film

00:01:17.537 --> 00:01:19.510
was Alfred Hitchcock's "Rope."

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:22.606
A more recent example is "Birdman."

00:01:22.606 --> 00:01:25.097
"1917" pushes it a step further

00:01:25.097 --> 00:01:26.788
with its persistent action

00:01:26.788 --> 00:01:29.060
and the constantly changing terrain.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.263
It never uses the same location twice.

00:01:32.263 --> 00:01:34.305
Who was up for this daunting task?

00:01:34.305 --> 00:01:36.890
Cinematographer Roger Deakins.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.542
Deakins earned a reputation as one of the

00:01:38.542 --> 00:01:40.710
greatest cinematographers alive today

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.110
thanks to his masterful work

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.970
on movies like "Skyfall" and "Sicario."

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.830
He earned the best cinematography Oscar

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.930
for "Blade Runner 2049."

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.763
Roger Deakins: I mean,
this is not a sort of style

00:01:50.763 --> 00:01:53.062
of filmmaking that is right for very film.

00:01:53.062 --> 00:01:54.840
It's very a particular film.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.490
Narrator: Director Sam
Mendes wanted to create

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.880
an immersive experience
and put the audience

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.270
in the shoes of a British
soldier fighting in World War I.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.610
Getting the camera right
up in the soldiers' faces

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.760
as they moved through
long, narrow trenches

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.890
was a major aspect of that.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:11.470
First off, because of all the long takes

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.240
the crew would be
shooting, "1917" required

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.270
even more preparation
than the average movie.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:21.130
For example, the crew built
models of every single set,

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.930
like this farmhouse that
the two soldiers visit.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.890
Here is the model they built,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.350
and here's how it appeared in the movie.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:29.620
The models helped the crew see

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.100
how the overall scene should look.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.620
Take the scene where Schofield

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.460
runs through a destroyed city.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.910
It was lit up by flares in the sky.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.020
Because the flares were
the main source of lighting

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.890
for certain shots in this scene,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.300
the crew needed to time exactly how long

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.600
the flares would be in the air.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.200
So they tested it on a
mini-model of the city

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.962
with a light contraption that traced

00:02:50.962 --> 00:02:53.140
which directions the shadows would move

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:56.440
and how light would fall
through the buildings' windows.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.870
Seeing the landscapes and sets in person

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.240
was just as important for getting

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.770
the entire continuous shot down.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:06.000
The art department built
realistic sets from scratch.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.270
Each set needed to be able to
accommodate the camera's path.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.140
For example, the barbed
wire the two soldiers

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.760
walk through in no man's
land had angled pathways

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.670
so the camera could get through.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.500
Ramps allowed the camera
to pass from no man's land

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.738
into the lower German trenches.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.058
Meanwhile, tunnel entrances had to be

00:03:25.058 --> 00:03:26.324
the exact right length

00:03:26.324 --> 00:03:29.300
to accommodate the camera and their cuts.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.460
One window was built to come apart

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.860
so that the camera could pass through it.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.840
Perhaps most impressive of all

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.450
was the 5,200 feet of trenches
the crew had to build.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.080
As some of the most action-packed
and challenging shots

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.030
happen in these narrow pathways,

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.890
it was crucial to measure everything.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.880
Every scene had to be the
exact length of the land.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.930
So, if a scene and shot
lasted eight minutes,

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.670
it had to take exactly that long

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.420
to get through that specific area.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.517
In an interview with Vox, Mendes said

00:03:59.517 --> 00:04:01.270
they wouldn't build a set until they knew

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.410
exactly how long it should be.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.910
This required a lot of rehearsing.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.990
While all films require rehearsals,

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:12.470
the process for "1917" was
much longer and more rigorous.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.200
According to Deakins, on most films,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.920
these rehearsals can take
place the day of shooting.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.836
But for "1917,"

00:04:19.836 --> 00:04:22.736
walkthroughs were
happening well in advance.

00:04:22.736 --> 00:04:24.990
They started rehearsing
at Shepperton Studios

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.530
near London and then moved to the sets.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.960
Every single line of dialogue

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.070
had to be rehearsed on location.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.440
The whole process of blocking
and rehearsing scenes

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.860
went on for four months.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.030
Once they had gotten the lay of the land,

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.550
measured everything out,

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.680
and made sure every actor knew their lines

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:43.750
and hit their marks,

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.160
it was time to start shooting.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.330
There was another crucial step
in pulling off this illusion.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:52.770
The camera could never move
backward, only forward,

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.670
as the characters had a starting point

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:58.412
and a fixed destination
and could never go back.

00:04:58.412 --> 00:05:00.112
One solution for that:

00:05:00.112 --> 00:05:03.404
letting the camera move 360 degrees.

00:05:03.404 --> 00:05:05.770
This allowed the crew to
constantly move forward

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:09.520
and follow the characters without
making a visible hard cut.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.470
Productions of this size
call for heavy equipment.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.570
But how was the crew
supposed to deal with cameras

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.400
that needed to be mobile?

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.740
The solution: a new model
of high-definition camera

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.810
that was much lighter
and smaller than usual.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.710
This gave Deakins the
same great image quality

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.040
but also allowed for more portability

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.180
and for the camera to
more quickly be attached

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.060
to something like a crane or a drone.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.740
A heavier camera would not have worked

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.640
with the crew's stabilizers,

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.870
which were crucial for
when the camera operators

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:41.340
had to walk and run after the soldiers.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.460
Deakins: Creatively, Sam
and I talked about it.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:45.830
We wanted the camera to be quite steady.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:47.840
We didn't want a jerky camera and stuff.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:48.800
Narrator: One can understand

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.800
why the camera needed to be light,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.810
as there are cameras running along wires

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.880
to capture shots like this one,

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.800
where Schofield jumps
across a broken bridge.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.710
The camera also traveled on wires

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:01.720
for its trip through no man's land.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.070
It could very easily be
unhooked from these wires

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:06.178
by a camera operator.

00:06:06.178 --> 00:06:08.610
Schofield's journey downriver was captured

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:10.430
mostly by a crane.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.130
A part of the sequence
in which the character

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.640
drops down an 18-foot set of waterfalls

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.123
was shot by attaching
the camera to a drone.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:21.445
The constant movement created
another big challenge:

00:06:21.445 --> 00:06:22.955
lighting a scene.

00:06:22.955 --> 00:06:25.110
They did use some artificial lighting,

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.000
like this 50-foot-tall light tower

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.150
that was dimmed to
create the color of fire

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:32.200
and meant to light up the
entire ruined village.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.360
However, if they put
lights in the trenches,

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.400
they might accidentally show up on screen

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:40.760
during one of the movie's
many 360-degree shots.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.380
So they relied mostly on natural light.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.020
Cloudy days were more
optimal than sunny days,

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.910
as sunny days meant more shadows.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:49.740
Deakins: I mean, I'm looking
at all my weather apps

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.800
to see when it's gonna cloud over.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.540
And then, is that cloud
gonna last long enough

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.210
to do a five-minute take?

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.730
Narrator: And because the
movie took place in real time,

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:00.880
the lighting had to look consistent.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.810
Even a little too much sun in the sky

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.360
meant production had to
shut down for the day.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.530
Deakins needed to know not
just how shots would look,

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.950
but how they would be stitched
together in post-production.

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:14.960
Deakins and Mendes found
subtle ways to make those cuts

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.680
without the audience knowing.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.856
This would also help
editor Lee Smith immensely.

00:07:19.856 --> 00:07:22.580
Sometimes an object would block the frame.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.730
Shots where characters
traveled through doorways

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:28.100
or entered a bunker also
allowed for easier cuts.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:30.550
Take this shot, where
the two main characters

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.310
walk through a black curtain.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:35.670
Other shots required even
cleverer transitions.

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.330
In one thrilling sequence,

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.200
Schofield runs through a burning city

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.780
and jumps off a ledge into a river.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.270
If you look at the same
shot behind the scenes,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.570
you'll see George running
through that same city

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:48.240
and jumping off the ledge.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.710
But instead of a river,
he jumps onto a mat.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:53.690
With the help of some visual effects,

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:56.190
the editors were able to
seamlessly stitch together

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:59.340
the character jumping off the
ledge and into the rapids.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.970
And to capture him even
getting to that point,

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:03.517
the camera crew had to make sure they were

00:08:03.517 --> 00:08:06.990
completely in sync with
the actor's every movement.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:09.280
Some parts of the
mazelike sequence involved

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:12.930
chasing after him with a camera
attached to a motorcycle.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.530
For the big leap, they
needed to attach a camera

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:18.402
to a longer, more flexible crane.

00:08:18.402 --> 00:08:20.330
But nothing quite matched the challenge

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:22.230
of pulling off the climatic sequence

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.570
in which Schofield runs alongside a trench

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.240
in the middle of battle.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.680
Getting this shot was a huge endeavor.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.330
Deakins: We started on
a 50-foot technocrane

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.990
that was armed in from,
say, as we're facing George,

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:36.259
from the right-hand side.

00:08:36.259 --> 00:08:38.770
And then it climbs up the trench with him

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:41.420
as the camera's booming out on the arm.

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:44.380
He hesitates at the top of the trench.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:46.860
Then the camera's taken
off that technocrane,

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:50.580
'cause the camera's basically
on a stabilized head

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.400
called a stable eye, surprise, surprise.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.728
And they hook it onto another technocrane.

00:08:56.728 --> 00:08:59.680
And that technocrane is on the
back of a tracking vehicle,

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:01.960
and that's already slowly moving back.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:04.980
So, as they hook it on, it's all one move.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.430
That moves back; George
speeds up and starts running.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:13.090
Meanwhile, the two grips
that did that are in costume.

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:16.430
So they find a way around
to be able to come across

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:19.270
the back of the shot,
and you don't see them

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:21.340
until that point, in costume.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:22.613
And they were quite glad,

00:09:22.613 --> 00:09:25.940
'cause they got money for
being extras that day.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:27.860
Narrator: Once the
camera was on the truck,

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:29.550
it had to follow George as he ran

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.076
for about a quarter of a mile.

00:09:32.076 --> 00:09:33.925
Meanwhile, they had explosions

00:09:33.925 --> 00:09:35.700
going on in the background.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.000
According to Deakins,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.040
they only had enough
explosions for four takes.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:42.930
And thanks to their tireless
rehearsals and walkthroughs,

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.340
they were able to pull it off on time.

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:47.020
And while everything
they did while shooting

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.846
had to be so precise,

00:09:48.846 --> 00:09:51.170
there was still room for accidents.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:53.360
As George is running out of the trench,

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:55.810
you'll see him bump into some extras.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:57.510
That wasn't planned.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:59.510
The actor bumped into them by accident,

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.090
and they just kept the camera rolling.

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:04.260
Had they called "cut" right away,

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:05.876
this great, genuine moment

00:10:05.876 --> 00:10:07.751
capturing the chaos of the war

00:10:07.751 --> 00:10:10.241
would never have made it into the movie.

